#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173088} :activity/id #uuid "be5d259b-c5cc-5faf-98aa-a40df75803b1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050508 :topic/id #uuid "4f46c12e-9985-5c0f-879f-f155a81c2351" :topic/math-academy-id 3386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173252 :tutorial/content "Consider the surface integral of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\n \nover the parametric surface $S,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 2v, \\: -u \\big\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is the unit square in the $uv$-plane given by\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\: | \\: 0\\leq u\\leq 1,\\: 0\\leq v\\leq 1\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the surface integral of $f$ over $S.$\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the surface integral of $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=-u} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(2v)^{2}+(-u)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+8\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5620ba9b-3795-5fdb-bc39-1699fb66c6b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7623 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "44c6f181-59d4-55f2-b782-d9635d1f8d52" :step/math-academy-id 44238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172130 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171710 :question/id #uuid "38dc8ce4-246d-54bd-a400-48c801d9387f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12344"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048384}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419401}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"} :step/id #uuid "c731cc78-fb9c-5b8e-b331-827b3178f676" :step/math-academy-id 44239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171711 :question/id #uuid "fce43073-3f48-5e12-a5c0-ecef3a31a57a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12346"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b529430-3406-506c-b67d-c067dfee44d5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418478}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"} :step/id #uuid "59e45b34-814a-5cf5-96db-f904f40aca38" :step/math-academy-id 44243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173256 :tutorial/content "Consider a continuous scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ and a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ defined for $(u,v) \\in D,$ where $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ \n\nIf our surface is \"sufficiently smooth,\" we can define the **surface integral** of $f$ over $S.$ Let's discuss how we might construct such an integral.\n\nFor the sake of simplicity, let's assume that our region $D$ is rectangular. We start by creating a partition $P$ of $D$ in the usual way:\n\n$$\nP = \\left\\{u_0, u_1, \\ldots, u_m\\right\\} \\times \\left\\{v_0, v_1, \\ldots, v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nOur partition is shown schematically below.\n\n![](images/6a/6a15c7b587503c82a57d0734c5bc5fda990824ad162724236868461cd92e4fbe.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v$ of the small rectangle highlighted in the diagram corresponds to a particular patch $S_{ij}$ of area $\\delta S_{ij}$ on our surface. \n\n![](images/1a/1a8774b7aa366d3328b6f81cfe7315b7784a1f5783f14c1f19f83a459cef5434.png)\n\n \n\nLet $P_{ij}^*$ be a point on the surface within the patch $S_{ij}.$ We consider the product \n\n$$\nf(P_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nIf we compute this product for all patches and add them together, we get the Riemann sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n f(P_{ij}^*)\\cdot\\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $\\delta u, \\delta v\\to 0$ gives our definition of the surface integral of $f$ over $S,$ which we denote as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFrom previous discussions, we know that the area of the small patch can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\delta S_{ij} \\approx \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta u\\delta v = \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta A\n$$\n\nIt follows that we can calculate our surface integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThis formula is also valid when $D$ is non-rectangular. \n\nLet's see an example of applying this in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "31cee43f-e940-5add-90d5-831ffcaaafc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2f4c811a-01f9-53a2-b82d-05cacf81451d" :step/math-academy-id 44475 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173080}}] :activity/title "Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172188}]}
